/* ---------- Section B questions ----------
   Midterm (Topics 1 to 7): short answers, 5 marks each.
   Final (Topics 8 to 14): short essays, 10 marks each.
   Each topic has one Knowledge (K) and one Application (A) question.
   guide: shown with the question. Says HOW to build the answer, not WHAT the answer is.
   model: the model answer, hidden in a closed dropdown.
   marks: [part, marks, what the marker looks for]. Marks must add up to the paper's essayMarks.
   from: optional release date "YYYY-MM-DD", as for MCQs. */
BANK.essays=[
/* ===== Midterm short answers (5 marks) ===== */
{id:"m1k",t:1,b:"K",q:"Explain what customer value and customer satisfaction mean. Then explain how the two ideas help a company build long-term relationships with its customers.",
 guide:["Define customer-perceived value in one or two sentences. Think about benefits, costs and competitors.","Define customer satisfaction. Think about what the customer expected and what they actually got.","Explain the link: how do value and satisfaction lead to loyalty and repeat buying?","Add one short Maldivian example, such as a café or a guesthouse.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Customer-perceived value is the customer's judgement of all the benefits and all the costs of an offer, compared with competitors' offers. Customers do not judge value exactly. They act on what they believe they get for what they pay.","Customer satisfaction depends on how well the product performs compared with what the buyer expected. If performance falls short, the customer is unhappy. If it matches, the customer is satisfied. If it goes beyond, the customer is delighted.","When customers feel they get high value and are satisfied, they buy again, stay loyal and tell others. This builds a long-term relationship and increases customer lifetime value.","For example, a guesthouse in Maafushi that gives clean rooms, friendly service and a free snorkelling trip at a fair price creates high value. Guests who are delighted come back and recommend it to friends."],
 marks:[["Customer value defined",1,"Benefits and costs, compared with competitors."],["Customer satisfaction defined",1,"Performance compared with expectations."],["Link to relationships",2,"Explains how value and satisfaction lead to loyalty and repeat buying."],["Example",1,"A relevant example."]]},
{id:"m1a",t:1,b:"A",q:"A phone repair shop in Malé has many customers who visit once but very few who come back. Explain how the shop could use customer relationship management to turn one-time customers into loyal customers.",
 guide:["Start with a one-line definition of customer relationship management (CRM).","Think about what the shop could learn about each customer, and how it could keep in touch.","Suggest two or three specific actions. For each, say how it builds loyalty.","Link your ideas to customer value or satisfaction.","End with one sentence on why keeping customers matters for profit."],
 model:["Customer relationship management is the overall process of building and keeping profitable customer relationships by delivering superior customer value and satisfaction.","First, the shop can record each customer's name, phone model and past repairs. Next time the customer visits, staff can greet them by name and know their history, which makes service faster and more personal.","Second, it can keep in touch. For example, it can send a Viber message when a repair is ready, and a reminder after six months to check the battery.","Third, it can reward loyalty, for example 10% off the third repair or a free screen protector for returning customers.","These actions raise customer value and satisfaction, so customers come back and recommend the shop. Keeping a customer usually costs less than finding a new one, so loyal customers increase profit."],
 marks:[["CRM defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Practical actions",3,"Two or three specific CRM actions for the shop, each explained."],["Link to loyalty and profit",1,"Explains why the actions build loyalty and profit."]]},
{id:"m2k",t:2,b:"K",q:"Explain the four types of business in the BCG growth-share matrix. For each type, state the strategy a company usually uses.",
 guide:["Start by naming the two measures the matrix uses (one on each axis).","Give each of the four types its own short point: where it sits on the matrix, and what it means.","For each type, add the usual strategy: build, hold, harvest or divest.","You may draw a small 2 by 2 box if it helps, but still explain in words.","Keep it short. About 7 minutes."],
 model:["The BCG growth-share matrix places each business unit by market growth rate and relative market share.","Stars have high share in a high-growth market. They need heavy investment to keep up with growth. The usual strategy is to build, so that they become cash cows later.","Cash cows have high share in a low-growth market. They are established and need less investment, so they produce cash for the company. The usual strategy is to hold, or to harvest some cash for other businesses.","Question marks have low share in a high-growth market. They need a lot of cash just to hold their share. Managers must decide which to build into stars and which to drop.","Dogs have low share in a low-growth market. They may make enough to support themselves but have little future. The usual strategy is to harvest or divest."],
 marks:[["The two measures",1,"Market growth rate and relative market share."],["Four types explained",2,"Star, cash cow, question mark and dog, each placed correctly."],["Strategy for each",2,"A suitable strategy for each type."]]},
{id:"m2a",t:2,b:"A",q:"A chain of three guesthouses on Maafushi is preparing its marketing plan. Explain how the owners could use a SWOT analysis, giving one realistic example for each part of the SWOT.",
 guide:["Explain what SWOT stands for, and which parts are inside the company and which are outside.","Give one example for each of the four parts. Make each example specific to Maafushi guesthouses.","Check: strengths and weaknesses must be things the guesthouses control. Opportunities and threats come from outside.","End by explaining how the owners would use the SWOT in their plan."],
 model:["A SWOT analysis assesses the company's strengths and weaknesses, which are internal, and the opportunities and threats it faces, which are external.","Strength: the guesthouses are close to the beach and have very good online reviews.","Weakness: the rooms are small and the Wi-Fi is slow, which guests often mention.","Opportunity: tourist arrivals from India and China are growing, and more travellers want budget island stays.","Threat: many new guesthouses are opening on Maafushi, so price competition is rising.","The owners should use their strengths to take the opportunities, for example by promoting their beach location and reviews to Indian travellers. They should also fix weaknesses such as Wi-Fi before the threats become worse."],
 marks:[["SWOT explained",1,"Internal and external parts made clear."],["Four examples",3,"A realistic example for each part (deduct for internal/external mix-ups)."],["Use in the plan",1,"How the owners would use the analysis."]]},
{id:"m3k",t:3,b:"K",q:"Explain any FOUR actors in a company's microenvironment. For each one, explain how it can affect the company's ability to serve its customers.",
 guide:["Start with a one-line definition of the microenvironment.","Choose four actors. Give each its own short point: what it is, then how it affects the company.","Use one Maldivian business as your example the whole way through, such as a supermarket or resort.","Do not include macroenvironment forces (such as economic or technological). They do not count here."],
 model:["The microenvironment is the actors close to the company that affect its ability to serve its customers.","Suppliers provide the resources the company needs. If a resort's food supplier delivers late because of rough seas, the resort cannot serve the meals guests expect.","Marketing intermediaries help the company promote, sell and deliver its products. A resort depends on travel agents and booking websites to reach guests.","Competitors offer similar products. A resort must offer more value than nearby resorts, or guests will choose them.","Customers are the most important actors. The resort must understand what honeymooners, families and divers each want.","Publics, such as the media and the local island council, can also help or harm the company's reputation and ability to operate."],
 marks:[["Microenvironment defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Four actors explained",4,"Four correct actors, each with how it affects the company (1 mark each)."]]},
{id:"m3a",t:3,b:"A",q:"A Maldivian travel agency notices two changes: tourist arrivals from India are growing fast, and more travellers now book everything on mobile apps. Identify the macroenvironment force behind each change, and explain how the agency should respond to each one.",
 guide:["Name the force for each change. Check that it is a macroenvironment force.","For each change, explain what it means for the agency's customers.","Then suggest one specific marketing response for each change.","Use separate paragraphs for the two changes."],
 model:["The growth in Indian tourists is a change in the demographic environment, because it concerns the size and make-up of the population the agency serves. It may also link to the economic environment, as more Indian families can now afford to travel.","The agency should respond by targeting this group. It could create packages that suit Indian families and honeymooners, offer vegetarian meal options, and promote them on platforms popular in India.","The move to mobile booking is a change in the technological environment.","The agency should respond by making its website work well on phones, adding WhatsApp booking, and partnering with popular travel apps. If it does not, travellers will book with competitors who are easier to use."],
 marks:[["Demographic force identified",1,"Correct force for the first change."],["Response to first change",1.5,"Specific marketing response, explained."],["Technological force identified",1,"Correct force for the second change."],["Response to second change",1.5,"Specific marketing response, explained."]]},
{id:"m4k",t:4,b:"K",q:"Explain the difference between primary data and secondary data. Give one advantage and one limitation of each, with an example.",
 guide:["Define each type of data in one sentence.","For each type, give one advantage and one limitation. Think about cost, speed and how well the data fits the problem.","Add one Maldivian example of each type of data.","End with one sentence on which type researchers usually start with, and why."],
 model:["Primary data is information collected for the specific purpose at hand. Secondary data is information that already exists somewhere, collected for another purpose.","An advantage of primary data is that it fits the exact problem and is up to date. A limitation is that it costs more and takes longer to collect. For example, a café surveys its own customers about a new menu.","An advantage of secondary data is that it is quick and cheap to get. A limitation is that it may be old, not accurate enough, or not fit the problem exactly. For example, a guesthouse uses tourist arrival figures from the tourism ministry.","Researchers usually start with secondary data, because it is cheaper, and collect primary data only when the secondary data is not enough."],
 marks:[["Both types defined",1,"Correct definitions."],["Advantages and limitations",2,"One advantage and one limitation of each (0.5 each)."],["Examples",1,"One relevant example of each."],["Conclusion",1,"Which to start with, and why."]]},
{id:"m4a",t:4,b:"A",q:"A Maldivian juice company wants to know which of three new flavours island shoppers prefer before it launches one. Recommend a research approach and a contact method, and explain how the company should choose its sample.",
 guide:["Recommend ONE research approach (observation, survey or experiment). Explain why it suits this problem.","Recommend ONE contact method (mail, telephone, personal, or online). Think about where island shoppers are and whether they need to taste the juice.","Explain the sample: who should be in it, how many, and how they should be chosen.","Keep it practical and specific to the Maldives."],
 model:["The company should use experimental research. It could give shoppers the three flavours to taste in the same conditions and record which one they choose. Changing only the flavour shows the cause of the preference, which is better than just asking opinions.","The best contact method is personal interviewing, because shoppers need to taste the juice. Staff could set up tasting stands in supermarkets in Malé and in shops on two or three larger islands. Short online follow-up questions could be added.","The sample should represent the target shoppers. It should include adults of different ages, from Malé and from the atolls, who buy juice regularly. A few hundred people would be enough. The company should choose them randomly at the stores, for example every fifth shopper, so the results are not biased."],
 marks:[["Research approach",2,"Suitable approach, with a reason."],["Contact method",1.5,"Suitable method for tasting and island shoppers."],["Sample",1.5,"Who, how many, and how chosen."]]},
{id:"m5k",t:5,b:"K",q:"Explain the four types of consumer buying behaviour. Give an example of each.",
 guide:["Name the two things the four types depend on: how involved the buyer is, and how different the brands are.","Give each type its own short point: when it happens, and an example.","Use everyday Maldivian examples, such as rice, a phone, biscuits or a car.","About 7 minutes."],
 model:["The four types depend on how involved the buyer is and how big the differences between brands are.","Complex buying behaviour happens when the buyer is highly involved and brands differ a lot. For example, a family in Hulhumalé buying a car compares many models carefully.","Dissonance-reducing buying behaviour happens when involvement is high but brands seem similar. For example, someone buying a sofa chooses fairly quickly, then looks for reasons to feel good about the choice.","Habitual buying behaviour happens when involvement is low and brand differences are small. For example, buying the usual brand of salt or rice without thinking.","Variety-seeking buying behaviour happens when involvement is low but brands differ clearly. For example, trying a different brand of biscuits each week."],
 marks:[["Basis of the four types",1,"Involvement and brand differences."],["Four types explained",2,"Each type correctly explained (0.5 each)."],["Examples",2,"A relevant example for each (0.5 each)."]]},
{id:"m5a",t:5,b:"A",q:"A new sports drink brand wants young people in Malé to buy it. Explain how TWO social or cultural factors could influence their buying decision, and how the brand could use each factor in its marketing.",
 guide:["Choose two factors from these: culture, subculture, social class, reference groups, opinion leaders, family, roles and status.","Define each factor in one sentence.","Explain how it affects young people in Malé when they choose a drink.","Suggest one marketing action that uses each factor."],
 model:["Reference groups are groups that people compare themselves with, or want to belong to. Young people in Malé are strongly influenced by friends, football teams and online communities. The brand could sponsor local futsal tournaments so the drink is seen as part of the sports group.","Opinion leaders are people whose skills, knowledge or personality influence others. A popular Maldivian athlete or fitness influencer on Instagram can shape what young fans buy. The brand could work with a well-known national football player who posts about drinking it after training.","Culture also matters. Healthy living and fasting during Ramadan affect when and what people drink. The brand could promote the drink for re-hydration after training and after iftar, with halal labelling."],
 marks:[["First factor",2.5,"Defined, linked to young buyers in Malé, with a marketing action."],["Second factor",2.5,"Defined, linked to young buyers in Malé, with a marketing action."]]},
{id:"m6k",t:6,b:"K",q:"Explain the four main steps in designing a customer value-driven marketing strategy.",
 guide:["Name the four steps in the right order. The first two choose customers; the last two decide how to serve them.","Give each step a short definition in your own words.","Use one Maldivian business as an example running through all four steps.","About 7 minutes."],
 model:["The first step is segmentation: dividing a market into smaller groups of buyers with different needs, characteristics or behaviours. For example, a resort company divides tourists into honeymooners, families and divers.","The second step is targeting: evaluating each segment's attractiveness and choosing one or more segments to serve. The resort chooses honeymooners from Europe.","The third step is differentiation: actually making the market offer different, to create superior customer value. The resort offers private overwater villas and sandbank dinners.","The fourth step is positioning: arranging for the offer to hold a clear, distinctive and desirable place in the minds of target customers. The resort is positioned as “the most romantic island in the Maldives”."],
 marks:[["Segmentation",1.25,"Defined and illustrated."],["Targeting",1.25,"Defined and illustrated."],["Differentiation",1.25,"Defined and illustrated."],["Positioning",1.25,"Defined and illustrated."]]},
{id:"m6a",t:6,b:"A",q:"A new gym is opening in Hulhumalé. Recommend a targeting strategy for the gym, explain why it suits the gym, and write a short positioning statement for it.",
 guide:["Name the targeting strategies, then choose ONE for the gym.","Explain your choice using the gym's size, resources and competition.","Describe the target customer clearly.","Write a positioning statement: To (target segment) who (need), our (brand) is (concept) that (point of difference).","Check that the point of difference is something rivals do not offer."],
 model:["A new gym has limited money and space, so concentrated (niche) marketing suits it best. It should aim at one segment it can serve very well instead of trying to please everyone.","Its target could be young working women in Hulhumalé aged 20 to 35. Many feel uncomfortable in crowded gyms and have little time after work.","Focusing on this group lets the gym design its space, class times and staff around their needs, and compete with larger gyms that serve everyone.","Positioning statement: To busy young women in Hulhumalé who want to stay fit in a comfortable space, FitHaven is the women-only gym that offers 45-minute classes with female trainers, open early and late."],
 marks:[["Targeting strategy chosen",1.5,"A suitable strategy, with a reason linked to the gym."],["Target customer described",1.5,"A clear, specific segment."],["Positioning statement",2,"Follows the structure, with a clear point of difference."]]},
{id:"m7k",t:7,b:"K",q:"Explain the four types of consumer products. Give a Maldivian example of each, and explain how the marketing differs between them.",
 guide:["Name the four types. Think about how customers shop for each one.","For each type: a short definition, a Maldivian example, and one way the marketing is different (price, distribution or promotion).","Keep each point short. About 7 minutes."],
 model:["Convenience products are bought often and quickly with little comparison, such as rice, bread and phone top-ups. They are priced low and sold in many outlets, with mass promotion.","Shopping products are bought less often, and customers compare quality, price and style, such as phones and clothes. They are sold in fewer outlets, with more personal selling and advertising.","Specialty products have unique features or strong brand identity, and buyers make a special effort to buy them, such as a luxury watch or a stay at a famous resort. They are sold in very few outlets, with carefully targeted promotion.","Unsought products are those customers do not know about or do not normally think of buying, such as life insurance or a funeral service. They need a lot of advertising and personal selling."],
 marks:[["Four types defined",2,"Convenience, shopping, specialty and unsought (0.5 each)."],["Examples",1,"Relevant Maldivian examples."],["Marketing differences",2,"How price, place or promotion differ for each."]]},
{id:"m7a",t:7,b:"A",q:"A well-known Maldivian tuna brand plans to launch a ready-to-eat tuna curry in a pouch. Explain the four brand development strategies, and recommend which one the company should use for this new product.",
 guide:["Name the four strategies. They depend on whether the product category is new or existing, and whether the brand name is new or existing.","Explain each in one or two sentences, applied to the tuna company.","Recommend one strategy. Use what you know about the brand's reputation and how close the curry is to its current products.","Mention one risk of your choice."],
 model:["Line extension uses an existing brand name for new forms, sizes or flavours in an existing category, for example a new chilli flavour of canned tuna.","Brand extension uses an existing brand name in a new product category. The tuna curry pouch would be a brand extension, because ready meals are a new category.","Multibrands means launching several brands in the same category, for example a second, cheaper tuna brand.","New brands means creating a completely new brand name, for example when the existing name does not fit the new product.","I recommend a brand extension. Customers already trust the brand's tuna quality, so the new curry will get attention and trial quickly at lower cost. The product is close to the core tuna range, so the name fits. The risk is that if the curry tastes poor, it could harm the image of the main tuna brand, so quality must be tested first."],
 marks:[["Four strategies explained",3,"Line extension, brand extension, multibrands, new brands, each applied."],["Recommendation",1.5,"A clear choice with a reason."],["Risk",0.5,"One risk of the chosen strategy."]]},

/* ===== Midterm bonus short answers, released 21 Oct 2026 19:12 Maldives time ===== */
{id:"m1k2",t:1,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"Explain the five marketing management orientations that a company can use to design its marketing strategy.",
 guide:["List the five orientations in the order the textbook gives them. There are five, so count them.","For each one, write one or two sentences: what does the company focus on?","Show how they move from the company's view (inside out) to the customer's and society's view (outside in).","One short example for one or two of them is enough.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["The production concept says consumers prefer products that are available and cheap, so the company focuses on making and distributing efficiently.","The product concept says consumers prefer products with the best quality, performance and features, so the company keeps improving the product. The risk is marketing myopia.","The selling concept says consumers will not buy enough unless the company sells and promotes hard. It focuses on making sales, not on long-term relationships.","The marketing concept says the company reaches its goals by knowing the needs and wants of target markets and satisfying them better than competitors. It works from the outside in.","The societal marketing concept says the company should also protect the long-term welfare of consumers and society, balancing profits, consumer wants and society's interests. For example, a juice brand that cuts sugar and uses recyclable bottles."],
 marks:[["Production concept",1,"Availability and low cost."],["Product concept",1,"Quality, performance and features."],["Selling concept",1,"Large-scale selling and promotion."],["Marketing concept",1,"Target customer needs, better than competitors."],["Societal marketing concept",1,"Balances company, consumer and society."]]},
{id:"m1a2",t:1,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"A new clothing brand in Malé sells mainly through Instagram to young customers. Explain how the brand could use customer-engagement marketing and consumer-generated marketing to build strong relationships with these customers.",
 guide:["Define customer-engagement marketing in one sentence. Think about making the brand part of customers' conversations and lives.","Suggest two engagement actions that suit Instagram and young people in Malé.","Define consumer-generated marketing in one sentence. Who creates the content?","Suggest one way the brand can encourage customers to create content, and note one risk.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Customer-engagement marketing means making the brand a real part of customers' conversations, experiences and lives, not just selling to them. The aim is direct and continuous contact.","The brand could reply quickly to comments and messages, and run polls where followers vote on new colours or designs. It could also post styling videos with local models, so customers feel part of the brand.","Consumer-generated marketing is brand content and messages created by customers themselves, such as photos, videos and reviews.","The brand could start a hashtag, for example #MaleStyle, and repost the best outfit photos each week with a small prize. This builds trust because young buyers trust other buyers more than adverts.","One risk is that the brand cannot fully control what customers post, so a bad review or poor photo may spread quickly. The brand should watch posts and respond politely."],
 marks:[["Engagement explained",1,"Brand as part of customers' conversations and lives."],["Two engagement actions",1.5,"Practical and suited to Instagram and young customers."],["Consumer-generated marketing explained",1,"Content created by customers."],["Action and risk",1.5,"One way to encourage customer content, plus one risk."]]},
{id:"m2k2",t:2,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"Explain the four Ps of the marketing mix. For each P, give one decision a Maldivian café would need to make.",
 guide:["Name the four Ps.","For each P, give a one-sentence definition from the textbook.","Then give one café decision for each P. Keep it specific: a real choice the owner must make.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Product is the mix of goods and services the company offers to the target market. The café must decide its menu, for example whether to add iced coffee and short eats.","Price is the amount customers must pay to get the product. The café must decide whether to price a coffee at MVR 35 like nearby cafés or higher, for a premium feel.","Place includes the company activities that make the product available to target customers. The café must decide its location, opening hours and whether to deliver through a food app.","Promotion means activities that communicate the merits of the product and persuade customers to buy it. The café could post daily specials on Instagram or offer a loyalty card."],
 marks:[["Product",1.25,"Definition and a café decision."],["Price",1.25,"Definition and a café decision."],["Place",1.25,"Definition and a café decision."],["Promotion",1.25,"Definition and a café decision."]]},
{id:"m2a2",t:2,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"A speedboat transfer company in Malé has written a new marketing plan to win more resort contracts. Explain how the company should manage this plan through marketing analysis, planning, implementation and control.",
 guide:["Name the four marketing management functions.","For each one, say what it means in one sentence.","Then apply each one to the speedboat company. What would the managers actually do?","Show that the four functions connect: control feeds back into analysis.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Marketing analysis means studying the company's situation, often with a SWOT analysis. The company would look at its strengths, such as new boats, and threats, such as rival operators and fuel prices.","Marketing planning means deciding on marketing strategies that help the company reach its goals. The company would choose which resorts to target, set prices for contracts and plan a sales visit schedule.","Marketing implementation means turning the plan into actions. Staff must actually visit resort managers, train crews in guest service and keep boats on time.","Marketing control means measuring results and taking action to fix problems. Each month the company would check how many contracts it won and how many trips ran late, and change the plan if targets are missed.","The functions work as a cycle: results from control give new information for the next round of analysis."],
 marks:[["Analysis",1.25,"Meaning and applied to the company."],["Planning",1.25,"Meaning and applied."],["Implementation",1.25,"Meaning and applied."],["Control",1.25,"Meaning, applied, and link back to analysis."]]},
{id:"m3k2",t:3,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"Explain what a public is. Then describe any FOUR types of publics that a company may face.",
 guide:["Give the textbook definition of a public in one sentence.","Choose four types of publics. Name each one clearly.","For each type, say who they are and how they can affect the company. A short example helps.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["A public is any group that has an actual or possible interest in, or impact on, an organisation's ability to achieve its goals.","Financial publics, such as banks and investors, affect the company's ability to get money. A guesthouse needs a bank loan to build new rooms.","Media publics, such as news websites, TV and bloggers, carry news and opinion about the company. A bad story can harm its image quickly.","Government publics are the authorities whose rules the company must follow, such as the tourism ministry and the island council.","Local publics include neighbourhood residents and community groups. A resort must keep good relations with people on nearby islands."],
 marks:[["Public defined",1,"Interest in, or impact on, the organisation's goals."],["Four types of publics",4,"1 mark each: correct name and how it affects the company."]]},
{id:"m3a2",t:3,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"A mobile phone shop in Malé faces two changes. The government introduces a new consumer protection rule that requires a written warranty with every phone, and the number of young families moving to Hulhumalé is growing fast. Identify the macroenvironment force behind each change and explain how the shop should respond.",
 guide:["Look at each change and decide which of the six macroenvironment forces it belongs to.","Name the force clearly for each change.","For each change, explain one effect on the shop and one marketing response.","Use the shop's situation. Avoid general answers that could fit any business.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["The warranty rule comes from the political and social environment, which includes laws and government agencies that limit businesses.","The shop must give a written warranty with every phone, which may add costs. It can turn this into a selling point by promoting 'every phone protected', and by training staff to explain warranty terms clearly.","The growth of young families in Hulhumalé is a change in the demographic environment, which covers population size, location and age.","This creates a new group of customers in a new place. The shop could open a small branch or a delivery service in Hulhumalé, and offer family phone bundles or student phones for older children."],
 marks:[["Force 1 identified",1,"Political and social (legal) environment."],["Response to force 1",1.5,"Effect on the shop and a sensible action."],["Force 2 identified",1,"Demographic environment."],["Response to force 2",1.5,"Effect on the shop and a sensible action."]]},
{id:"m4k2",t:4,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"Explain the three main research approaches for gathering primary data: observational, survey and experimental research. Give an example of each.",
 guide:["For each approach, write one sentence on what it is.","Say what kind of information each approach is best for.","Add one short Maldivian example for each.","Finish with one sentence comparing them.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Observational research gathers data by watching relevant people, actions and situations. It is good for finding out what people actually do. For example, a supermarket watches which aisles shoppers visit first.","Survey research asks people questions about their knowledge, attitudes, preferences and buying behaviour. It is best for descriptive information. For example, a gym emails members a short satisfaction questionnaire.","Experimental research uses matched groups of people, gives them different treatments, controls other factors and checks the differences in response. It is best for cause-and-effect information. For example, a café tests two prices for the same drink in two similar branches.","Observation shows behaviour, surveys show opinions, and experiments show causes."],
 marks:[["Observational research",1.5,"Meaning, what it suits, example."],["Survey research",1.5,"Meaning, what it suits, example."],["Experimental research",1.5,"Meaning, what it suits, example."],["Comparison",0.5,"One clear point of comparison."]]},
{id:"m4a2",t:4,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"A Maldivian airline collects large amounts of data from its booking website, mobile app and loyalty card. Explain what big data and marketing analytics are, and how the airline could use them to build better customer relationships. State one risk it must manage.",
 guide:["Define big data in one sentence.","Define marketing analytics in one sentence. What does it do with the data?","Give two specific ways the airline could use the results.","Name one risk, such as privacy, and say how the airline could manage it.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Big data means the very large and complex sets of data produced by today's information technologies, such as websites, apps and loyalty cards.","Marketing analytics means the tools, technologies and processes marketers use to dig out patterns in big data to gain customer insights and measure marketing performance.","The airline could find customers who fly to Colombo every month and send them a business travel bundle. It could also spot customers who have stopped flying and send them a personal offer to win them back.","One risk is customer privacy. If the airline misuses personal data or loses it in a data leak, customers will lose trust. It should ask for permission, protect the data and explain clearly how it is used."],
 marks:[["Big data defined",1,"Large, complex data from modern technology."],["Marketing analytics defined",1,"Tools to find patterns and insights."],["Two uses",2,"Specific to the airline and its customers."],["Risk",1,"Privacy or misuse, with a way to manage it."]]},
{id:"m5k2",t:5,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"Explain Maslow's hierarchy of needs. Give an example of how a Maldivian business could appeal to each level.",
 guide:["Start with one sentence: what does Maslow's theory say about how needs are arranged?","Name the five levels from the most basic to the highest.","For each level, give a short example of a business or product that appeals to it.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Maslow said human needs are arranged in a hierarchy, from the most pressing at the bottom to the least pressing at the top. People try to satisfy the most important need first.","Physiological needs are hunger and thirst. A bottled water brand appeals to this level.","Safety needs are security and protection. An insurance company or a sea ambulance service appeals to this level.","Social needs are belonging and love. A café that is a meeting place for friends appeals to this level.","Esteem needs are self-esteem, recognition and status. A luxury watch shop in Malé appeals to this level.","Self-actualisation needs are self-development and realisation. A diving course that lets people reach a new personal goal appeals to this level."],
 marks:[["Theory explained",1,"Needs arranged in order; most pressing first."],["Five levels with examples",4,"Correct order and a sensible example for each (0.8 each)."]]},
{id:"m5a2",t:5,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"A new resort in Lhaviyani Atoll needs to buy a water desalination plant before it opens. Once it is open, it will buy cleaning chemicals every month. Explain the three main types of business buying situations, and say which one fits each purchase.",
 guide:["Name the three buying situations.","Explain each in one or two sentences. Think about how much is new for the buyer.","Match the desalination plant to one situation and give a reason.","Match the monthly cleaning chemicals to one situation and give a reason.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["In a straight rebuy, the buyer reorders something without any changes, often from the same supplier.","In a modified rebuy, the buyer wants to change product details, prices, terms or suppliers.","In a new task, the buyer purchases a product or service for the first time. The cost and risk are higher, so more people take part and more information is gathered.","The desalination plant is a new task. The resort has never bought one, it is expensive and important, so engineers, the finance manager and the general manager will all compare suppliers carefully.","The monthly cleaning chemicals are a straight rebuy. The resort orders the same products from the same supplier each month with little thought, unless prices rise, which would make it a modified rebuy."],
 marks:[["Three situations explained",3,"Straight rebuy, modified rebuy, new task."],["Desalination plant",1,"New task, with a reason."],["Cleaning chemicals",1,"Straight rebuy, with a reason."]]},
{id:"m6k2",t:6,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"Explain the five requirements that make market segments useful. Use one example to show each requirement.",
 guide:["Name the five requirements. A memory aid helps: M, A, S, D, A.","For each one, write one sentence on what it means.","Add a short example for each, such as a segment that passes or fails the test.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Measurable: the size, buying power and profile of the segment can be measured. A guesthouse can count how many Italian divers visit each year.","Accessible: the segment can be reached and served. Young people in Malé can be reached through Instagram.","Substantial: the segment is large or profitable enough to serve. A segment of only ten people would not be worth a special product.","Differentiable: the segment responds differently to marketing. Honeymooners and families want different things from a resort.","Actionable: the company can design programmes to attract and serve the segment. A small café may not have the staff to serve a large corporate catering segment."],
 marks:[["Measurable",1,"Meaning and example."],["Accessible",1,"Meaning and example."],["Substantial",1,"Meaning and example."],["Differentiable",1,"Meaning and example."],["Actionable",1,"Meaning and example."]]},
{id:"m6a2",t:6,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"A new mid-range café is opening in Hulhumalé, close to several large cafés. Explain any THREE winning value propositions, then recommend one for the café and explain why.",
 guide:["Pick three of the five value propositions. Name them exactly.","For each, explain in one sentence what the customer gets for what price.","Recommend one for this café. Link your reason to the competition and the customers nearby.","Add one thing the café must do to deliver that proposition.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["More for more means offering the best product or service and charging a higher price to cover the higher cost.","More for the same means offering similar quality to competitors at a lower price than its value suggests, or more benefits at the same price.","The same for less means offering the same products as competitors but at a lower price, often through lower costs.","I recommend 'more for the same' for this café. It cannot easily beat the large cafés on price, and it is not a luxury brand. By charging the same as nearby cafés but giving more, such as free Wi-Fi, quiet study corners and fresh local snacks, it gives students and young workers a clear reason to choose it.","To deliver this, the café must keep its costs under control and make the extra benefits visible on its menu and Instagram."],
 marks:[["Three propositions explained",3,"1 mark each, correctly named and explained."],["Recommendation",1.5,"Clear choice linked to the café's situation."],["How to deliver",0.5,"One practical action."]]},
{id:"m7k2",t:7,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"Explain the five main decisions a company makes about individual products and services.",
 guide:["Name the five decisions. Count them before you write.","For each, write one sentence on what the company decides.","A short example for each, from one Maldivian product, makes the answer stronger.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["Product and service attributes: the company decides the quality, features, and style and design of the product. For example, a tuna brand decides how much salt to use and whether the can has an easy-open lid.","Branding: the company chooses a name, sign or symbol that identifies the product and makes it different from competitors.","Packaging: the company designs the container or wrapper. Good packaging protects the product and attracts buyers on the shelf.","Labelling: the label identifies the product or brand, describes it, and may show nutrition facts or a halal mark.","Product support services: the company adds services such as delivery, repairs or a customer hotline to improve the customer's experience."],
 marks:[["Attributes",1,"Quality, features, style and design."],["Branding",1,"Name, sign or symbol."],["Packaging",1,"Container and its roles."],["Labelling",1,"Identifies, describes, informs."],["Support services",1,"Services added to the product."]]},
{id:"m7a2",t:7,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:12:00+05:00",q:"A guesthouse on Dhigurah gets many online complaints about unfriendly staff. Using the service profit chain, explain how improving staff satisfaction could lead to more profit. Then explain how the owner could use internal marketing and interactive marketing.",
 guide:["Describe the links in the service profit chain, in order, from staff to profit.","Apply the chain to the guesthouse: what changes at each link?","Define internal marketing and give one action.","Define interactive marketing and give one action.","About 7 minutes. Half a page is enough."],
 model:["The service profit chain links service firm profits with employee and customer satisfaction. Satisfied and productive staff create greater service value. This leads to satisfied and loyal customers, which leads to healthy profits and growth.","At the guesthouse, if staff feel valued and well trained, they will be friendlier. Guests will enjoy their stay, write good reviews and come back, which brings more bookings and more profit.","Internal marketing means training and motivating staff who deal with customers, and support staff, to work as a team to satisfy customers. The owner could run a short guest service training and give a monthly reward for the best reviewed staff member.","Interactive marketing means training staff in the skill of serving customers, because service quality depends on the contact between buyer and seller. The owner could teach staff how to greet guests, handle complaints calmly and remember guests' names."],
 marks:[["Service profit chain",2,"Correct links from staff to profit, applied to the guesthouse."],["Internal marketing",1.5,"Meaning and an action."],["Interactive marketing",1.5,"Meaning and an action."]]},

/* ===== Final short essays (10 marks) ===== */
{id:"e8k",t:8,b:"K",q:"Explain the stages a company goes through to develop a new product, from first idea to full launch. For each stage, say what the company does and why it matters.",
 guide:["Open with one sentence on why companies use a step-by-step process for new products (think about risk and failure).","Name every stage in the correct order. Count them before you start writing, so you do not miss one.","Give each stage its own short paragraph or point: what the company does, then why it matters.","Use one simple Maldivian example and carry it through the stages, for example a new drink or a new tour.","End with one sentence on how the process keeps the customer at the centre."],
 model:["New products fail often and cost a lot of money. A clear process lowers this risk. Armstrong and Kotler describe eight stages.","1. Idea generation. The company searches for new ideas from staff, customers, competitors and suppliers. It needs many ideas to find a few good ones.","2. Idea screening. Poor ideas are dropped and good ones are kept. This saves money, because each later stage costs more.","3. Concept development and testing. The idea is turned into a product concept in customer words, and a group of target customers react to it. This shows whether people would really buy it.","4. Marketing strategy development. The company plans the target market, positioning, price, distribution and budget.","5. Business analysis. It checks expected sales, costs and profits against company goals.","6. Product development. The concept becomes a real product, and prototypes are tested for safety and quality.","7. Test marketing. The product and marketing programme are tried in a real market, for example one island or one store, to find problems before the full launch.","8. Commercialisation. The company launches the product fully, deciding when and where to launch.","For example, a Malé juice company could follow these steps for a new passion fruit drink and test it in Hulhumalé first. The process keeps customers involved at every stage, which makes success more likely."],
 marks:[["Stages named in the right order",3,"All eight stages, correct names and order."],["What happens at each stage",4,"A clear description of the company's actions at each stage."],["Why each stage matters",2,"Links stages to lowering risk and cost, and to customer needs."],["Structure and example",1,"A clear, organised answer with a relevant example."]]},

{id:"e8a",t:8,b:"A",q:"A Maldivian juice brand launched a passion fruit drink three years ago. Sales grew fast at first, but growth has now slowed and two rival brands have copied the drink. Identify the stage of the product life cycle the drink has reached. Explain the strategies the brand could use at this stage, and recommend the one you think would work best.",
 guide:["Start by naming the stage. Prove it with TWO clues from the question.","Describe in one or two sentences what usually happens to sales, profits and competition in this stage.","Explain the main strategy options for this stage. The textbook groups them into three types: think about the market, the product and the marketing mix.","Give a Maldivian example for each option, linked to the juice brand.","Finish with a clear recommendation and a reason. Say why it fits this brand better than the others."],
 model:["The drink is in the maturity stage of the product life cycle. Sales growth has slowed, and rivals have copied the product. Both are typical signs of maturity.","In maturity, sales level off, competition is strong and prices and profits come under pressure. The brand cannot just wait. It should try to give the product new life.","First, it can modify the market. It can find new users, such as resorts, cafés and guesthouses, or new uses, such as a mixer for mocktails. It can also encourage current users to drink more, for example by selling a family-size bottle.","Second, it can modify the product. It can improve the taste, launch a no-sugar version or add new flavours such as passion fruit and lime, to stand out from the copies.","Third, it can modify the marketing mix. It can run a sales promotion in supermarkets, move into more island shops, or use social media influencers in Malé to remind people of the original brand.","I recommend modifying the product with a no-sugar version. Many Maldivians are now more careful about sugar. A healthier version gives the brand a real difference from the copies and protects its price better than discounts would."],
 marks:[["Stage identified with evidence",2,"Maturity, supported by clues from the question."],["Features of the stage",2,"Sales, competition and profit pressure explained."],["Strategies explained",4,"Market, product and marketing mix options, each applied to the brand."],["Recommendation",2,"A clear choice with a reason linked to the case."]]},

{id:"e9k",t:9,b:"K",q:"Explain the three major approaches companies use to set prices. Discuss one strength and one weakness of each approach.",
 guide:["Begin with a one-line definition of price.","Name the three approaches. Use a separate paragraph for each one.","In each paragraph: what it means, how it works, one strength and one weakness.","Use a simple example with MVR prices to show at least one approach in action.","Finish by saying which approach puts the customer at the centre, and why that matters."],
 model:["Price is the amount of money charged for a product or service. Armstrong and Kotler describe three major pricing approaches.","Customer value-based pricing uses the buyer's view of value, not the seller's cost. The company first studies what customers value and then sets a price to match. A strength is that the price fits what customers will pay. A weakness is that customer value is hard to measure.","Cost-based pricing sets prices from the costs of making, distributing and selling the product, plus a fair return. Cost-plus pricing is the simplest form. For example, a shop that buys a T-shirt for MVR 100 and adds 50% sells it for MVR 150. A strength is that it is simple. A weakness is that it ignores demand and competitors' prices.","Competition-based pricing sets prices based on competitors' strategies, costs, prices and offers. A strength is that it keeps prices in line with the market. A weakness is that following rivals may not match the value the company gives.","Customer value-based pricing is the best starting point. Cost sets the floor and customer value sets the ceiling. Companies that understand value can avoid charging too little or too much."],
 marks:[["Three approaches named and defined",3,"Value-based, cost-based and competition-based, each explained."],["Strengths and weaknesses",4,"One clear strength and weakness for each approach."],["Example",2,"A relevant example, ideally with MVR figures."],["Conclusion",1,"Links pricing back to customer value."]]},

{id:"e9a",t:9,b:"A",q:"A new guesthouse in Thulusdhoo is about to open. The owner cannot decide whether to start with high room rates or low room rates. Explain the two pricing strategies for new products, and recommend which one the guesthouse should use.",
 guide:["Name and define the two new-product pricing strategies. Use one paragraph for each.","For each, explain the conditions that must be true for it to work (think about quality image, number of buyers, costs and competitors).","Apply each strategy to the guesthouse: what would the prices look like and what could go wrong?","Use facts about Thulusdhoo or local tourism (surfing, many guesthouses, price-aware travellers) to support your view.","End with a clear recommendation and a reason."],
 model:["New products can be priced with market-skimming pricing or market-penetration pricing.","Market-skimming pricing sets a high starting price to take the most revenue from customers who will pay more. It works when the product has a strong quality image, enough buyers want it at that price and competitors cannot easily enter. For the guesthouse, this would mean high rates aimed at guests who want something special.","Market-penetration pricing sets a low starting price to win many buyers and a large market share quickly. It works when the market is price-sensitive, costs fall as sales grow and the low price keeps rivals out. For the guesthouse, this would mean lower rates to fill rooms fast and get early reviews.","Thulusdhoo has many guesthouses and is popular with surfers and budget travellers who compare prices online. A new guesthouse has no reviews or reputation yet, so skimming would be hard to support.","I recommend market-penetration pricing at the start. Low rates will fill rooms, earn good online reviews and build word of mouth. Once the guesthouse has a strong rating, the owner can raise rates slowly or add value-added services such as surf trips."],
 marks:[["Both strategies defined",2,"Skimming and penetration explained correctly."],["Conditions for each",2,"When each strategy works."],["Application to the guesthouse",4,"Each strategy applied with local facts."],["Recommendation",2,"A clear, justified choice."]]},

{id:"e10k",t:10,b:"K",q:"Explain the functions that marketing channel members perform. Using these functions, explain why producers use intermediaries instead of always selling directly to consumers.",
 guide:["Start with a one-line definition of a marketing channel.","Explain why intermediaries exist: think about the number of contacts needed with and without them.","Explain the channel functions. The textbook splits them into functions that help complete a sale and functions that help carry out a sale. Use short points.","Give one Maldivian example of an intermediary doing one of these functions.","End with a sentence on what happens to the functions if an intermediary is removed."],
 model:["A marketing channel is a set of businesses that work together to make a product available to the final customer.","Producers use intermediaries because they are more efficient. Through their contacts, experience and size, intermediaries do more than most producers could do alone. For example, if three producers each sell directly to three customers, nine contacts are needed. With one distributor in the middle, only six are needed.","Some functions help complete transactions. Information: gathering facts about customers and competitors. Promotion: spreading persuasive messages. Contact: finding and talking with buyers. Matching: shaping the offer to buyers' needs, for example sorting and packaging. Negotiation: agreeing on price and terms.","Other functions help carry out completed transactions. Physical distribution: transporting and storing goods. Financing: paying for the costs of channel work, such as giving credit. Risk taking: accepting the risks of the channel work.","For example, an atoll distributor stores rice, breaks it into smaller lots and gives credit to island shops. These functions cannot be removed. If a producer cuts out an intermediary, it must do this work itself or pass it to someone else."],
 marks:[["Channel defined and need explained",2,"Definition and fewer-contacts efficiency idea."],["Transaction functions",3,"Information, promotion, contact, matching, negotiation."],["Physical functions",3,"Physical distribution, financing, risk taking."],["Example and conclusion",2,"Local example; functions must still be done."]]},

{id:"e10a",t:10,b:"A",q:"A tuna canning company in Felivaru wants to sell its canned tuna to households across all inhabited islands of the Maldives. Explain the steps the company should follow to design its marketing channel, and recommend a channel structure for it.",
 guide:["List the steps of channel design in order before you start (the textbook has four).","Use one paragraph per step. In each, say what the step means and apply it to the tuna company.","Think about Maldivian conditions: distance between islands, boats, monsoon, small island shops, Malé as a hub.","Consider how many outlets canned tuna needs, given how often people buy it.","End with a clear recommended structure (how many levels, which intermediaries) and why."],
 model:["Channel design has four steps: analysing consumer needs, setting channel objectives, identifying major alternatives and evaluating them.","Analysing consumer needs. Households want to buy canned tuna close to home, at a fair price and at any time. Most islands are small and far from Malé, so convenience matters most.","Setting channel objectives. The company should aim to reach every inhabited island with steady supply, at a cost that keeps the price affordable. It must also plan for monsoon delays.","Identifying major alternatives. The company must choose the types of intermediaries, such as atoll distributors and island shops, the number of outlets and each member's responsibilities. Canned tuna is a low-cost product bought often, so it needs intensive distribution.","Evaluating alternatives. Each option is judged on economic, control and adaptability criteria. Selling directly to every household would give control but cost far too much.","I recommend an indirect two-level channel. The company sells to atoll distributors, who sell to island shops, who sell to households. It can also sell directly to large supermarkets and resorts in Malé. Distributors hold stock near islands, which helps during the monsoon."],
 marks:[["Steps named in order",2,"Four channel design steps."],["Steps applied to the company",5,"Each step linked to the tuna company and Maldivian conditions."],["Recommendation",3,"A clear structure with levels and reasons."]]},

{id:"e11k",t:11,b:"K",q:"Explain the main marketing decisions a retailer must make. Use examples to show how each decision helps a retailer stand out from its competitors.",
 guide:["Start with a short definition of retailing.","First explain the strategy decisions: who the store serves and how it wants to be seen.","Then explain each part of the retail marketing mix in its own paragraph.","Give a different Maldivian store example for each decision, for example a supermarket, a bookshop or a clothing shop.","Finish with one sentence on why every decision must fit the target market."],
 model:["Retailing includes all activities involved in selling goods or services directly to final consumers for their personal use.","Segmentation, targeting, differentiation and positioning come first. A retailer must decide which customers it serves and how it wants them to see the store. A store that tries to serve everyone often serves no one well.","Product assortment and services. The retailer decides the range of products and the services it offers. A dive shop in Hulhumalé can stand out with a deep range of masks and fins and free equipment fitting.","Store atmosphere. This is part of the product offer. Lighting, music, scent and layout make shoppers feel a certain way. A café in Malé may use soft music and warm light to make people stay longer.","Price. Prices must fit the target market and positioning. A discount store keeps prices low, while a boutique charges more for quality and service.","Promotion. Retailers use advertising, personal selling, sales promotion, public relations and social media. A supermarket may post weekly deals on Instagram.","Place. Location is very important. A shop near a ferry terminal gets many passing customers.","Each decision must fit the target market, so that the whole store sends one clear message."],
 marks:[["Retailing defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Strategy decisions",2,"Segmentation, targeting, differentiation, positioning."],["Retail marketing mix",5,"Assortment and services, atmosphere, price, promotion, place."],["Examples",2,"Relevant examples showing how each decision helps a store stand out."]]},

{id:"e11a",t:11,b:"A",q:"A family-owned grocery shop in Hithadhoo has served its neighbourhood for twenty years. A large new supermarket has just opened nearby with lower prices and a wider range. Analyse how the family shop could use its retail marketing decisions to compete, and recommend the most important change it should make.",
 guide:["Start by comparing the two stores. What does the supermarket do better, and what can a small shop do better?","Choose a clear target customer and position for the family shop. Do not try to beat the supermarket on price.","Work through the retail marketing mix: assortment and services, price, promotion, place and atmosphere. Give one idea for each.","Link each idea to real conditions in Addu City, such as busy families, older customers, credit, or delivery by motorbike.","End with one clear recommendation and the reason it matters most."],
 model:["The new supermarket wins on low prices and a wide range. The family shop cannot match these. It can win on closeness, personal service and knowing its customers.","Targeting and positioning. The shop should target nearby families, older people and busy workers who want quick, friendly service. It should position itself as the trusted neighbourhood shop.","Assortment and services. It can stock everyday essentials and local products the supermarket does not carry, such as fresh local snacks. It can offer phone orders, home delivery by motorbike and monthly credit for regular customers.","Price. It should keep essentials fair, but not try to match every supermarket price.","Promotion. A Viber or WhatsApp group can share daily fresh stock and small loyalty rewards.","Place and atmosphere. It is already close to homes and open long hours. A clean, tidy store with friendly greetings adds to the feeling of trust.","I recommend phone ordering with home delivery as the most important change. It turns the shop's closeness and personal knowledge into a service the large supermarket cannot easily copy."],
 marks:[["Comparison of the two stores",2,"Strengths and weaknesses of each."],["Target and position",2,"Clear target customer and position."],["Retail mix applied",4,"Practical ideas across the mix, linked to the setting."],["Recommendation",2,"One justified change."]]},

{id:"e12k",t:12,b:"K",q:"Explain the five major tools of the promotion mix. Then explain why companies need integrated marketing communications (IMC) when they use many of these tools at once.",
 guide:["Start with a one-line definition of the promotion mix.","Explain each of the five tools in its own short paragraph: what it is and when it works best.","Give a quick Maldivian example for each tool.","Then define IMC, and explain the problem it solves (think about customers seeing many messages from many places).","Finish with one sentence on how IMC helps build the brand."],
 model:["The promotion mix, or marketing communications mix, is the blend of tools a company uses to communicate customer value and build relationships.","Advertising is any paid, non-personal presentation of ideas or products by an identified sponsor, such as a TV advert by a telecom company.","Sales promotion is a short-term incentive to encourage buying, such as a supermarket's buy-one-get-one-free offer.","Personal selling is personal presentation by the sales force to make sales and build relationships, such as a resort's sales manager meeting travel agents.","Public relations builds good relations with the company's publics by earning favourable publicity, such as a bank's beach clean-up covered by the news.","Direct and digital marketing engages carefully targeted customers through email, social media, mobile and websites, such as an airline's app offers.","Today customers see messages from many sources. If these messages conflict, the brand image becomes confused. Integrated marketing communications means carefully joining and coordinating all of these channels to deliver one clear, consistent and convincing message about the company and its products.","IMC makes every touchpoint tell the same story, which builds a stronger brand."],
 marks:[["Promotion mix defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Five tools explained",5,"Each tool explained with an example."],["IMC defined",2,"Coordination for one clear, consistent message."],["Why IMC is needed",2,"Many channels and conflicting messages explained."]]},

{id:"e12a",t:12,b:"A",q:"A new Maldivian domestic airline is launching flights between Malé and the southern atolls. Recommend a promotion mix for the launch, and explain how the airline can use integrated marketing communications to keep its message clear and consistent.",
 guide:["Start by stating the target audience(s) for the launch. There may be more than one group.","Recommend tools from the promotion mix. For each, say what the airline would do and why it suits the audience.","Think about what stage the airline is at: what must people learn first about a new airline?","Explain IMC: what one message should all tools share, and how will the airline make sure every channel says it?","End with a short summary of your mix and why it fits a new launch."],
 model:["The airline has two main audiences: island residents travelling to Malé for work, school and hospital visits, and travel agents and resorts who book tourists.","Advertising. Because the airline is new, it needs informative advertising: the routes, times and prices. Social media and local TV can reach residents across the atolls.","Sales promotion. Launch fares, such as a discount for the first month or a free extra bag, encourage people to try it.","Personal selling. A small sales team should visit resorts and travel agents to sign agreements, because these buyers book many seats.","Public relations. A first-flight event with island councils, covered by news sites, builds goodwill and trust.","Direct and digital marketing. An app and website for booking, with SMS reminders, make buying easy.","IMC means all these tools share one message, for example “Closer to home, on time”. The same logo, colours and promise should appear in adverts, the app, airport signs and staff uniforms. One person or team should check every message before it goes out.","This mix informs people first, encourages trial and builds long-term trust."],
 marks:[["Target audience",1,"Clear audience for the launch."],["Promotion mix recommended",5,"Suitable tools, each applied and justified."],["IMC explained and applied",3,"One clear message and how it is kept consistent."],["Summary",1,"Links the mix to the launch stage."]]},

{id:"e13k",t:13,b:"K",q:"Explain the steps of the personal selling process. For each step, describe what a good salesperson does.",
 guide:["Start with a one-line definition of personal selling.","Count the steps before you write, so you do not miss one. Keep them in the right order.","Give each step a short paragraph: its name, what the salesperson does, and why.","Use one Maldivian example and carry it through, for example selling a boat engine or a resort stay.","Finish by explaining which step builds the long-term relationship, and why."],
 model:["Personal selling is personal presentation by a company's sales force to make sales and build customer relationships. The selling process has seven steps.","1. Prospecting and qualifying. The salesperson finds possible customers and checks whether they have the need, money and authority to buy.","2. Pre-approach. The salesperson learns as much as possible about the prospect before the visit, such as their needs and who makes the decision.","3. Approach. The salesperson meets the buyer and starts the relationship well, with a good first impression and opening questions.","4. Presentation and demonstration. The salesperson tells the value story and shows how the product solves the customer's problem.","5. Handling objections. The salesperson asks questions to find the buyer's concerns and answers them, turning them into reasons to buy.","6. Closing. The salesperson asks for the order, for example by offering to arrange delivery.","7. Follow-up. After the sale, the salesperson checks that the customer is satisfied and plans for repeat business.","For example, an engine dealer selling to a fishing boat owner in Laamu would check the boat size first, explain fuel savings and visit after installation. Follow-up builds the long-term relationship, because it shows the company cares after it has the money."],
 marks:[["Personal selling defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Steps named in order",3,"All seven steps in the correct order."],["What happens at each step",4,"Clear actions for each step."],["Example and relationship",2,"Relevant example and link to long-term relationships."]]},

{id:"e13a",t:13,b:"A",q:"A new bottled water brand from Hulhumalé wants island shops to stock it and households to try it. Recommend sales promotion tools aimed at both shops and households. Explain how each tool supports the brand, and how the brand can avoid damaging its image with too many promotions.",
 guide:["Start with a short definition of sales promotion and its two target groups in this question.","Recommend two or three consumer promotion tools. For each: what it is, how the brand would use it, and why it helps.","Recommend one or two trade promotion tools for island shops in the same way.","Think about the risk of too many promotions. What happens to a brand's image and to customer habits?","End with a short plan for using promotion to build long-term customer relationships."],
 model:["Sales promotion is a short-term incentive to encourage the purchase or sale of a product. This brand needs consumer promotions for households and trade promotions for island shops.","Samples. The brand can give free small bottles at Hulhumalé ferry terminals and football matches. Trying the water is the best way to show its taste.","Price packs. A “6 bottles for the price of 5” pack encourages households to try more and store it at home.","Premiums. A free reusable cooler bag with a bulk purchase adds value and keeps the brand in view.","Allowances and free goods. Island shops can get one free case for every ten ordered in the first two months. This lowers their risk of stocking a new brand.","Display allowances. The brand can pay shops a small fee to place the water in the front fridge.","Too many promotions can make the brand look cheap and teach customers to wait for deals. The brand should run promotions for a limited time and link them to building the brand, for example a loyalty card that rewards regular buyers. This builds long-term relationships, not just short-term sales."],
 marks:[["Sales promotion defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Consumer promotions",3,"Suitable tools applied to households."],["Trade promotions",3,"Suitable tools applied to island shops."],["Risks and long-term view",3,"Image risks and how to use promotion to build relationships."]]},

{id:"e14k",t:14,b:"K",q:"Explain the principles of sustainable marketing. Give one example of a company practice for each principle.",
 guide:["Start with a one-line definition of sustainable marketing.","Count the principles before you write, so you do not miss one.","Give each principle its own short paragraph: what it means in your own words, then a practice that shows it.","Use Maldivian examples where you can, such as resorts, fishing, retail or telecoms.","Finish by explaining why following these principles is good for the company too, not only society."],
 model:["Sustainable marketing means socially and environmentally responsible marketing that meets the present needs of consumers and businesses, while also protecting the ability of future generations to meet their needs. Armstrong and Kotler describe five principles.","Consumer-oriented marketing. The company plans and organises its marketing from the customer's point of view. For example, a telecom company studies how island users use data before designing packages.","Customer-value marketing. The company puts most of its resources into activities that build real customer value, such as better service rather than more advertising.","Innovative marketing. The company keeps looking for real product and marketing improvements. A resort that turns waste glass into products for sale is an example.","Sense-of-mission marketing. The company defines its mission in broad social terms, such as a fishing company that says its purpose is to protect ocean life for the next generation.","Societal marketing. The company makes decisions by thinking about consumers' wants, the company's needs, consumers' long-run interests and society's long-run interests, aiming for desirable products.","Following these principles builds trust and loyalty, which helps the company earn profits over the long term."],
 marks:[["Sustainable marketing defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Five principles explained",5,"Each principle explained clearly."],["Examples",3,"One relevant practice for each principle."],["Conclusion",1,"Benefit to the company explained."]]},

{id:"e14a",t:14,b:"A",q:"A popular fast-food chain in Malé sells cheap fried meals in plastic boxes with plastic cups and straws. Evaluate its practices using the principles of sustainable marketing and the societal classification of products. Recommend two changes that would make the chain more sustainable while keeping it profitable.",
 guide:["Start with a short definition of sustainable marketing.","Use the societal classification grid to place the chain's main products. Explain your choice with the two dimensions (satisfaction now, benefit in the long run).","Weigh both sides: what the chain does well for customers, and where it may harm customers or society. Think about health and waste in a small island country.","Link your points to named sustainable marketing principles.","Recommend two specific changes. For each, say how it helps society AND how it can still make money."],
 model:["Sustainable marketing meets today's needs while protecting the ability of future generations to meet theirs.","The chain does well on some points. Its meals are cheap, fast and tasty, so it is consumer-oriented for busy workers and students in Malé.","However, its fried meals give high satisfaction now but may harm health in the long run. In the societal classification of products, they are pleasing products. Plastic boxes, cups and straws add to waste in a country with limited land, where much rubbish goes to Thilafushi or the sea. This fails the societal marketing principle, which considers society's long-run interests.","Overall, the chain meets short-term wants well but does not yet act sustainably.","First, it should add healthier meals, such as grilled fish wraps with local vegetables. These can be desirable products: tasty now and healthy later. Health-aware customers may pay a little more, which protects profit.","Second, it should replace plastic with paper or reusable packaging and give a small discount to customers who bring their own cup. This cuts waste, improves the brand's image and attracts young customers who care about the ocean."],
 marks:[["Concept defined",1,"Sustainable marketing defined."],["Products classified",2,"Correct use of the societal grid with reasons."],["Evaluation",3,"Both sides weighed with principles and a judgement."],["Two recommendations",4,"Specific changes that help society and keep profit."]]},
/* ===== Final bonus short essays (10 marks), released 21 Oct 2026 19:56 Maldives time ===== */
{id:"e8k2",t:8,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Explain the stages of the product life cycle. For each stage, describe what usually happens to sales, profits and competition, and what the company's main marketing aim should be.",
 guide:["Open with a one-sentence definition of the product life cycle.","Count the stages before you write. The textbook includes the stage before launch, so check you have them all.","Give each stage its own short paragraph. Use the same three points each time: sales, profits, competition.","Then add the main marketing aim for that stage in one sentence.","Use one simple Maldivian product as your example all the way through.","End with one sentence on why not every product follows the same curve."],
 model:["The product life cycle (PLC) is the course of a product's sales and profits over its lifetime. The textbook describes five stages.","1. Product development. The company finds and develops the idea. Sales are zero and investment costs keep rising. The aim is to prepare a product that customers really want.","2. Introduction. The product is launched. Sales grow slowly and profits are low or negative, because launch costs are high. There are few competitors. The aim is to build awareness and get people to try the product.","3. Growth. Sales rise quickly. Profits improve. New competitors enter, attracted by the chance to make money. The aim is to win as much market share as possible, for example by adding features and new outlets.","4. Maturity. Sales growth slows and then levels off. There are many competitors, so prices and profits come under pressure. Most products are in this stage. The aim is to defend share and give the product new life by modifying the market, the product or the marketing mix.","5. Decline. Sales and profits fall. Some competitors leave. The aim is to decide whether to keep the product, harvest it or drop it.","For example, a Maldivian bottled iced coffee would sell slowly at launch, grow fast once cafés and shops stock it, then level off when rivals copy it. Not every product follows this curve. Styles, fashions and fads have their own patterns, so managers use the PLC as a guide, not a rule."],
 marks:[["Stages named in order",2,"All five stages, including product development."],["Sales, profits and competition",4,"Correct pattern for each stage."],["Marketing aim for each stage",3,"A suitable aim or strategy for each stage."],["Example and structure",1,"A relevant example and a clear, organised answer."]]},

{id:"e8a2",t:8,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"A tour company in Malé wants to create a new half-day trip for tourists who have a few hours to spare before their flight. Explain how the company could use customer-centred and team-based new product development. Then recommend how it should test the new trip before a full launch.",
 guide:["Define customer-centred new product development in one sentence. Then show two ways the company could involve tourists in its ideas.","Define team-based new product development. Name the teams or roles inside a tour company that should work together, and say what each adds.","Explain why each approach lowers the risk of failure.","For testing, think about two stages: testing the idea (before the trip exists) and testing the real trip in the market. Say what the company would do at each.","End with a clear recommendation and one reason."],
 model:["Customer-centred new product development focuses on finding new ways to solve customer problems and create more satisfying experiences. The tour company should start with the tourists. It could ask hotel guests and airport transfer drivers what tourists want to do in their last hours. It could also read online reviews of Malé tours to find what visitors enjoy and what they dislike, such as long waits or heavy bags.","This matters because new products fail most often when they do not meet a real need. Tourists with a flight to catch need a short, safe, well-timed trip with somewhere to leave their bags.","Team-based new product development means people from different departments work closely together and overlap the steps, instead of passing the idea from one department to the next. The guides know what tourists enjoy. The boat crew know travel times to the airport. The sales team know the hotels and travel agents. Finance can check costs early. Working together saves time and avoids late surprises, such as a trip that cannot reach the airport on time.","For testing, the company should first use concept testing. It can describe the trip in a few lines, with a price, and ask a group of tourists whether they would buy it and what they would change.","Next, it should use test marketing. It can run the trip for one month through two partner hotels only, then check bookings, guest reviews and timing problems.","I recommend this two-step test. Concept testing is cheap and shows interest. A small test market shows real behaviour and real problems. Together they cut the risk before the company spends money on a full launch through all its partners."],
 marks:[["Customer-centred development",3,"Defined and applied to the tour company, with clear actions."],["Team-based development",3,"Defined, with the roles involved and why it helps."],["Testing recommendation",3,"Concept testing and test marketing explained and applied."],["Justification",1,"A clear reason for the recommendation."]]},

{id:"e9k2",t:9,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Explain the FIVE product mix pricing strategies. Give a Maldivian example of each, and explain why a company would use it.",
 guide:["Open with one sentence on why pricing is harder when a product is part of a product mix.","Count five strategies before you write. Think about product lines, extras, items needed to use the main product, leftover materials, and groups of products sold together.","For each strategy: a one-sentence meaning, a short Maldivian example, and one reason a company would use it.","Use a separate short paragraph for each strategy.","Keep examples simple: shops, resorts, phones, boats, cafés."],
 model:["When a product is part of a product mix, the company looks for a set of prices that makes the most profit on the whole mix, not on one product alone. The textbook gives five product mix pricing strategies.","Product line pricing sets price steps between the products in a line. For example, a guesthouse charges different rates for standard rooms, sea view rooms and beach villas. It helps customers see the difference in quality, and lets the company serve guests with different budgets.","Optional-product pricing prices extra products that customers can choose to add to the main product. For example, a dive centre charges extra for an underwater camera rental or a night dive. It keeps the main price attractive while extras add profit.","Captive-product pricing prices products that must be used with the main product. For example, a water filter seller sells the filter unit cheaply but makes profit on replacement cartridges. Customers keep coming back for the captive product.","By-product pricing sets a price for by-products that are left over from making the main product. For example, a tuna factory sells fish waste to animal feed makers. It turns waste into income and can help keep the main product's price low.","Product bundle pricing combines several products and offers them at a lower price. For example, a resort sells a package with room, meals and a snorkelling trip. It encourages customers to buy products they might not buy on their own."],
 marks:[["Introduction",1,"Why product mix pricing aims at profit on the whole mix."],["Five strategies explained",5,"Each strategy correctly explained (1 mark each)."],["Examples and reasons",4,"A relevant example and a reason for using each strategy (0.8 each)."]]},

{id:"e9a2",t:9,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Fuel costs have risen sharply, and a speedboat transfer company in Malé must raise its fares. In the same month, a rival company cuts its fares. Explain how the company should raise its prices without losing customers' trust. Then evaluate the ways it could respond to the rival's price cut, and recommend the best response.",
 guide:["Paragraph 1: explain why a price increase can be risky for customer relationships.","Paragraph 2: explain how the company should raise its prices. Think about how it explains the increase, and other ways to cover higher costs.","Paragraph 3: before responding to the rival, list the questions the company should ask itself.","Paragraph 4: explain the main response options. The textbook gives several, from doing nothing to launching a cheaper brand. Weigh one strength or weakness of each.","Paragraph 5: recommend ONE response, and give a reason linked to this company."],
 model:["A price increase can make customers feel the company is taking advantage of them, which harms trust. But if costs rise and the company does not raise prices, its profits fall.","The company should explain the reason clearly. It can post a short message to guests, hotels and travel agents saying that fuel costs have gone up and showing the change. It should give agents some notice before the new fares start. It can also look for other ways to cover costs, such as charging a small fee for extra bags or offering cheaper fares on less busy trips, so the main fare rises less.","Before it responds to the rival's cut, the company should ask why the rival cut its fares, whether the cut is short-term, and how many of its own customers are likely to switch.","It has several options. First, it could hold its price. This protects profit, but it may lose some price-sensitive guests. Second, it could cut its own fares. This keeps market share but lowers profit, at a time when fuel costs are high. Third, it could keep its price but raise perceived value, for example with better on-time service, cold water and help with bags. Fourth, it could improve quality and raise price further, aiming at premium guests. Fifth, it could start a cheaper, basic service as a fighter brand.","I recommend holding the price and raising perceived value. Most transfer guests care most about safety and being on time for their flight. A price war would cut profit just when fuel costs are high. Clear communication and better service will keep loyal hotels and guests."],
 marks:[["Raising prices well",3,"Clear communication and other ways to cover costs."],["Questions before responding",1,"Reasons for the cut, how long it lasts, and its effect."],["Response options evaluated",4,"At least three options, each with a strength or weakness."],["Recommendation",2,"A clear choice with a reason linked to the case."]]},

{id:"e10k2",t:10,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Explain the THREE types of vertical marketing system. Give an example of each, and explain how a vertical marketing system can reduce channel conflict compared with a conventional channel.",
 guide:["Open with a short definition of a conventional distribution channel and a vertical marketing system. Show the difference.","Name the three types. For each: what holds the channel together, and one example.","The three types differ in what gives the leader its control. Check each paragraph names that clearly.","Then explain the link to channel conflict. Why does a conventional channel have more conflict?","End with one sentence that sums up the benefit."],
 model:["A conventional distribution channel has one or more independent producers, wholesalers and retailers. Each one is a separate business trying to make the most profit for itself, even if this lowers profit for the whole system. A vertical marketing system (VMS) is a channel where producers, wholesalers and retailers act as one unified system.","In a corporate VMS, one company owns the different stages of production and distribution. For example, a bottled water company in the Maldives owns its factory, its delivery boats and its own shops. Ownership gives it control.","In a contractual VMS, independent firms at different levels join through contracts to get better results than they could alone. The most common form is the franchise. For example, an international fast-food brand gives a Maldivian company a franchise to run its outlets in Malé.","In an administered VMS, leadership comes from the size and power of one of the channel members, not from ownership or contracts. For example, a very large supermarket chain can tell smaller suppliers how to pack and deliver their goods.","In a conventional channel, no member has enough control over the others, so conflict over goals, roles and rewards is common. A VMS reduces this conflict because one member leads, roles are clear, and all members work toward the same goal. This makes the channel more efficient and better for customers."],
 marks:[["Conventional channel and VMS defined",2,"Clear definitions and the difference."],["Three types explained",3,"Corporate, contractual and administered, with what gives control."],["Examples",2,"A relevant example of each type."],["Link to channel conflict",3,"Explains why a VMS reduces conflict."]]},

{id:"e10a2",t:10,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"A small coconut oil maker in Fuvahmulah sells only through its own shop on the island. It now wants to reach resorts, supermarkets in Malé and online buyers. Recommend a multichannel distribution system for the company. Explain the risks of using several channels, and how the company should manage its channel members.",
 guide:["Define a multichannel distribution system in one sentence.","Design the system: for each customer group in the question, name the channel and the channel level.","Explain two risks. Think about channel conflict and control.","Explain how to manage channel members. The textbook groups this into selecting, managing and motivating, and evaluating.","Finish with a short justification of why your system suits a small company on a far island."],
 model:["A multichannel distribution system is one where a single firm sets up two or more marketing channels to reach one or more customer segments.","For resorts, the company should use a one-level channel through a Malé food importer that already supplies resorts. For Malé supermarkets, it can also use a one-level channel, selling directly to two or three large supermarket chains. For online buyers, it can sell directly through its own website and Instagram, with delivery by cargo boat and a courier in Malé. The island shop stays as a direct channel for local customers.","There are two main risks. First, channel conflict. The importer may be unhappy if the company sells online at a lower price than the importer can offer. Second, loss of control. Once the oil is with the supermarkets, the company cannot fully control how it is displayed or priced.","To manage these channels, the company must first select good partners: an importer with strong resort contacts, a good payment record and care for quality. Next, it should manage and motivate them by treating them as partners, sharing sales information, giving fair margins and agreeing clear roles, such as resorts through the importer only. Finally, it should evaluate them regularly against sales targets, delivery times and customer feedback.","This system suits a small company far from Malé. It uses partners who already have customers and transport, while the online channel builds the brand directly. Clear roles and fair prices across channels will keep conflict low."],
 marks:[["Multichannel system designed",3,"Defined, with a suitable channel for each customer group."],["Risks explained",2,"Channel conflict and control, applied to the company."],["Managing channel members",3,"Selecting, motivating and evaluating, applied."],["Justification",2,"Why the system suits this company."]]},

{id:"e11k2",t:11,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Explain the main functions that wholesalers perform. Use these functions to explain why small shops on Maldivian islands usually buy from wholesalers instead of directly from manufacturers.",
 guide:["Open with a one-sentence definition of wholesaling.","Explain at least FIVE wholesaler functions. Give each one a short point: what the wholesaler does.","Then apply the functions to an island shop. Pick the functions that matter most for a small shop far from Malé, and explain why.","Think about money, transport, storage, risk and information.","End with one sentence on the value wholesalers add to the channel."],
 model:["Wholesaling includes all the activities involved in selling goods and services to those buying for resale or business use.","Wholesalers perform several functions. Selling and promoting: their sales staff help manufacturers reach many small shops at low cost. Buying and assortment building: they choose items and build a range that small shops need. Bulk breaking: they buy in large amounts and sell in smaller lots. Warehousing: they hold stock, which lowers the costs and risks for suppliers and shops. Transportation: they deliver faster because they are closer to the buyers. Financing: they give credit to customers. Risk bearing: they absorb risk by taking ownership of goods and bearing the cost of theft, damage and spoilage. Market information: they tell suppliers and customers about competitors, new products and prices.","A small shop in Thaa Atoll cannot order a full container of rice or soap from a factory abroad. A wholesaler in Malé breaks bulk, so the shop can buy just a few bags. The wholesaler also builds an assortment, so the shop can buy rice, oil, soap and biscuits in one order instead of dealing with dozens of manufacturers.","Transport and warehousing matter even more on islands. The wholesaler stores stock in Malé and sends it by cargo boat, so the shop does not need a big storeroom. Credit is also important: the wholesaler may let the shop pay after it sells the goods.","In this way wholesalers add value by making goods available on far islands at lower cost and lower risk than direct buying would."],
 marks:[["Wholesaling defined",1,"Correct definition."],["Functions explained",5,"At least five functions, each correctly explained (1 mark each)."],["Application to island shops",3,"Shows clearly why island shops depend on these functions."],["Conclusion",1,"The value wholesalers add."]]},

{id:"e11a2",t:11,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"A bookshop in Malé is losing sales to online shopping. Evaluate how the bookshop could use omni-channel retailing and shopper marketing to win customers back. Recommend TWO actions and explain why they would work.",
 guide:["Define omni-channel retailing and shopper marketing in one sentence each.","Explain the problem from the customer's side. Why are readers buying online?","Evaluate omni-channel retailing for this shop: one benefit and one limit or cost.","Evaluate shopper marketing for this shop: one benefit and one limit or cost.","Recommend TWO specific actions. For each one, explain how it brings customers back to the shop.","Finish with one sentence on what makes a physical shop different from a website."],
 model:["Omni-channel retailing creates a seamless shopping experience across all the channels a customer uses, in the store, online and on mobile. Shopper marketing focuses the whole marketing process on turning shoppers into buyers as they get close to the point of sale.","Readers buy online because it is easy, has a wide range and can be done from home. The bookshop cannot beat large online sellers on range, so it must offer what they cannot.","Omni-channel retailing would let customers check if a book is in stock on their phone, reserve it, and collect it the same day. The benefit is speed and convenience, which large foreign online sellers cannot match in Malé. The limit is cost: the shop needs a simple stock system and staff time to keep it up to date.","Shopper marketing would make the store a better place to choose and buy. Staff recommendations, displays for exams and school terms, and a quiet reading corner can turn browsers into buyers. The benefit is that it uses the shop's main strength, which is the physical experience. The limit is that it only works if people come into the store in the first place.","My first recommended action is a WhatsApp and Instagram order service with in-store pick-up and island delivery. It brings online shoppers into the shop and serves the atolls. My second action is a weekly student and family event, such as a story hour or exam revision table, linked to in-store displays. It gives people a reason to visit and buy while they are there.","A website can sell books. Only a physical shop can offer advice, community and the pleasure of browsing, and these actions build on that."],
 marks:[["Concepts defined",2,"Omni-channel retailing and shopper marketing."],["Evaluation",4,"Benefits and limits of both ideas for this bookshop."],["Two recommended actions",4,"Specific, practical actions with reasons (2 marks each)."]]},

{id:"e12k2",t:12,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Explain the steps a company follows to develop effective marketing communications, from identifying the target audience to collecting feedback.",
 guide:["Count the steps before you write. There are six. List them in order in your plan.","Give each step its own short paragraph: what the company decides, and why it matters.","For the objectives step, show you know the buyer-readiness stages.","For the message step, mention the types of appeal.","Use one simple example and carry it through all six steps, such as a new café or a new ferry service.","End with one sentence on how feedback leads back to the start."],
 model:["Developing effective marketing communications has six steps.","1. Identify the target audience. The company decides who it is talking to, such as current users, possible buyers or decision makers. The audience affects what, how, when and where the message is said. A new café near MNU would target students and staff.","2. Determine the communication objectives. The company decides what response it wants. Buyers move through six buyer-readiness stages: awareness, knowledge, liking, preference, conviction and purchase. A new café must first build awareness.","3. Design a message. The message should get attention, hold interest, create desire and lead to action. The company chooses an appeal: rational (quality, value), emotional (feelings) or moral (what is right). The café could use a rational appeal: “good coffee at a student price”.","4. Choose the media. The company chooses personal channels, such as word of mouth and staff, and non-personal channels, such as social media, print and events. The café could use Instagram and student influencers.","5. Select the message source. People are more likely to believe a source they trust. The café could use a popular student from MNU to post about it.","6. Collect feedback. The company checks how many people saw the message, how they felt and whether they bought. The café can count the people who show its Instagram post at the till.","Feedback shows what to change, so the company returns to the earlier steps and improves the next campaign."],
 marks:[["Six steps in order",2,"All six steps named in the correct order."],["Each step explained",6,"What the company does at each step and why (1 mark each)."],["Example",1,"A relevant example used through the steps."],["Structure",1,"A clear, organised answer."]]},

{id:"e12a2",t:12,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"A new Maldivian ice cream brand made from local fruits is planning its first advertising campaign. Explain the major decisions the brand must make to develop its advertising programme, and recommend what it should do at each decision.",
 guide:["Name the four major advertising decisions in order. Count them before you write.","Objectives: is this new brand informing, persuading or reminding? Say why.","Budget: name one budget method and explain why it suits a new brand.","Message and media: describe a creative idea, an execution style, and the media you would use. Think about reach and frequency.","Evaluation: explain how the brand would measure communication effects and sales effects.","Make every point specific to this brand and to the Maldives."],
 model:["The textbook describes four major advertising decisions: setting objectives, setting the budget, developing the strategy (message and media), and evaluating the campaign.","Objectives. As a new brand, the main objective is to inform: to tell people the brand exists, that it uses local fruits such as mango, papaya and coconut, and where to buy it. This builds primary demand.","Budget. I recommend the objective-and-task method. The brand defines its goals, such as 60% awareness among young people in Malé in three months, works out the tasks needed and costs them. This is better than the affordable method, which might give too little money to launch properly.","Message and media. The creative idea could be “A taste of our islands”. A slice-of-life style would work well, showing friends sharing ice cream on the Malé artificial beach after school. The main media should be Instagram and TikTok, because young people use them every day, plus posters in shops that sell the brand. To launch, the brand needs high reach first, then enough frequency so people see the ad several times.","Evaluation. The brand should measure communication effects, such as views, shares and awareness in a short online survey. It should also measure sales effects, comparing sales in shops before and after the campaign.","Together, these decisions help the brand spend its limited money well and learn what to improve next time."],
 marks:[["Four decisions named",1,"Objectives, budget, message and media, evaluation."],["Objectives",2,"Suitable objective with a reason linked to a new brand."],["Budget",2,"A suitable method with a reason."],["Message and media",3,"Creative idea, execution style and media choice, applied."],["Evaluation",2,"Communication and sales effects."]]},

{id:"e13k2",t:13,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Explain the main steps in managing a sales force. For each step, explain how it helps the company build strong customer relationships.",
 guide:["Open with one sentence on what sales force management means.","Count the steps before you write. The textbook gives six, from designing the strategy and structure to evaluating.","Give each step its own paragraph: what the company does, then how it helps customer relationships.","For the structure step, name the main types of sales force structure.","Use one simple example, such as a Maldivian building supply company.","End with one sentence linking good sales force management to customer value."],
 model:["Sales force management means analysing, planning, carrying out and controlling sales force activities. It has six main steps.","1. Designing sales force strategy and structure. The company chooses a territorial, product, customer or complex structure, and decides the size of the sales force. A clear structure means each customer knows who to call and gets a salesperson who understands them.","2. Recruiting and selecting salespeople. Careful selection finds people who are honest, good listeners and keen to help. Good salespeople build trust with customers.","3. Training salespeople. Training teaches product knowledge, customer needs and selling skills. Well-trained salespeople solve customers' problems instead of just pushing products.","4. Compensating salespeople. Pay is usually a mix of fixed salary, variable pay such as commission, expenses and benefits. Pay should reward customer satisfaction and long-term sales, not only quick sales.","5. Supervising and motivating salespeople. Managers help salespeople plan their time and set sales quotas, and motivate them with recognition and rewards. Motivated salespeople give better service.","6. Evaluating salespeople. Managers use sales reports and customer feedback to judge performance. This shows where salespeople need help, so customers get better service.","For example, a building supply company in Malé would assign one salesperson to resorts and train them in resort needs. Good sales force management turns salespeople into trusted partners who create value for customers."],
 marks:[["Six steps named",2,"All six steps in a sensible order."],["Each step explained",5,"What the company does at each step."],["Link to customer relationships",2,"How each step helps build relationships."],["Example and structure",1,"A relevant example and a clear answer."]]},

{id:"e13a2",t:13,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"A Maldivian company sells and services water desalination plants for resorts. A sale can take many months. Explain how the company's salesperson should use the selling process to win a new resort as a customer, and how value selling would help build a long-term relationship.",
 guide:["Choose the steps of the selling process that matter most for a long, high-value sale. You do not need to explain all seven in detail.","For prospecting and the pre-approach, say how the salesperson finds and learns about the resort.","For the presentation and handling objections, think about what a resort manager worries about: cost, breakdowns, water quality.","For closing and follow-up, explain how the sale continues after the plant is installed.","Define value selling. Explain why selling on value is better than selling on price for this product."],
 model:["The selling process is the set of steps a salesperson follows when selling. For a large, long sale like a desalination plant, each step must be done with care.","Prospecting and qualifying. The salesperson finds resorts that may need a plant, such as new resorts being built or older resorts with ageing equipment. They check that the resort has the budget and the authority to buy.","Pre-approach. Before the first meeting, the salesperson learns about the resort: its size, guest numbers, current water supply and problems. They find out who decides, such as the general manager and the chief engineer.","Approach, presentation and demonstration. The salesperson listens first, then shows how the plant solves the resort's problems. They could take the engineer to see a working plant at another resort.","Handling objections. The manager may say the plant is too expensive. The salesperson should show the full cost over ten years, including lower fuel use and fewer breakdowns, and explain the service plan.","Closing and follow-up. The salesperson asks for the order when the resort is ready. After installation, they visit regularly, check performance and train staff. Follow-up is where the long-term relationship is built.","Value selling means showing and delivering the customer value of the product, and getting a fair return for that value, instead of competing on price. For a resort, safe and reliable water is vital, because guests cannot go without it. When the salesperson proves value, such as lower running costs and fast repairs, the resort will stay loyal and buy service contracts and future plants."],
 marks:[["Early steps applied",3,"Prospecting, qualifying and pre-approach applied to a resort."],["Presentation and objections",2,"How the salesperson shows value and answers concerns."],["Closing and follow-up",2,"How the sale closes and the relationship continues."],["Value selling",3,"Defined and linked to a long-term relationship."]]},

{id:"e14k2",t:14,b:"K",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Explain how consumerism and environmentalism push companies toward more sustainable marketing. Give examples of how a company can respond to each movement.",
 guide:["Define consumerism in one sentence. Then explain what rights consumers want. The textbook lists rights for buyers as well as sellers.","Give one or two examples of how a company can respond to consumerism.","Define environmentalism in one sentence. Explain what environmental sustainability means for a company.","Give one or two examples of how a company can respond to environmentalism.","End by linking both movements to sustainable marketing."],
 model:["Consumerism is an organised movement of citizens and government agencies to improve the rights and power of buyers in relation to sellers.","Traditional sellers' rights include the right to launch any product that is not harmful, to charge any price it sets fairly, and to use any promotion that is not misleading. Consumerism adds buyers' rights: the right not to be sold a harmful product, the right to receive honest information, the right to be protected from questionable products and marketing, and the right to influence products and practices in ways that improve quality of life.","A company can respond by giving clear labels with full prices and ingredients, keeping fair return policies, and answering complaints quickly. For example, a Malé electronics shop can show the full price with tax and give a clear warranty.","Environmentalism is an organised movement of concerned citizens, businesses and government agencies to protect and improve people's current and future living environment. Environmental sustainability means developing strategies that both protect the environment and produce profits for the company.","A company can respond by reducing pollution and waste, using recyclable or refillable packaging, saving energy and designing greener products. For example, a Maldivian water company can offer large refillable bottles to cut plastic waste on islands.","Both movements push companies to look beyond quick sales. When companies protect buyers' rights and the environment, they meet today's needs without harming future generations, which is the goal of sustainable marketing."],
 marks:[["Consumerism explained",2,"Defined, with buyers' rights."],["Response to consumerism",2,"Suitable company actions with an example."],["Environmentalism explained",2,"Defined, with environmental sustainability."],["Response to environmentalism",2,"Suitable company actions with an example."],["Link to sustainable marketing",2,"Clear link between the movements and sustainable marketing."]]},

{id:"e14a2",t:14,b:"A",from:"2026-10-21T19:56:00+05:00",q:"Customers complain that a mobile phone shop in Malé shows false “was” prices, pushes buyers to sign expensive payment plans on the spot, and sells chargers that break within weeks. Analyse these practices using the social criticisms of marketing. Recommend how the shop can use the principles of sustainable marketing to rebuild trust.",
 guide:["Name the criticism behind each of the three practices. Use the textbook's terms.","For each one, explain why it harms customers and why it also harms the shop in the long run.","Choose two or three sustainable marketing principles that fit this shop best. Say what each one means.","For each principle, recommend one specific action for the shop.","End with one sentence on how fixing these practices helps profit over time."],
 model:["The first practice is deceptive pricing. Showing a false “was” price makes buyers think they are getting a big saving when they are not. The second is high-pressure selling. Pushing customers to sign payment plans on the spot makes them buy things they did not plan to buy and may not afford. The third is selling shoddy or unsafe products. Chargers that break quickly waste customers' money, and a poor charger can even be a fire risk.","These practices may raise sales today, but they harm the shop later. Customers who feel cheated do not return, and they share bad reviews on social media. In a small market like Malé, word spreads fast. The shop may also face action from consumer protection authorities.","The shop should use consumer-oriented marketing, which means seeing and organising its activities from the customer's point of view. It should show only real prices, explain the full cost of payment plans in writing, and give customers time to decide.","It should use customer-value marketing, putting its resources into building real value. It should stop selling poor chargers, stock good quality accessories and offer a clear warranty.","It should also use sense-of-mission marketing, defining its purpose in broad terms, such as “helping Maldivians stay connected safely and fairly”, and train staff to follow it.","These changes may lower some quick sales, but they build trust, loyal customers and good reviews, which bring more profit over time."],
 marks:[["Criticisms identified",3,"Deceptive pricing, high-pressure selling and shoddy or unsafe products, each linked to the case."],["Analysis of harm",2,"Effects on customers and on the shop."],["Principles applied",4,"Two or three principles, each with a specific action."],["Conclusion",1,"Link to long-term profit."]]}
];
